            <ArticleTitle>Age estimation by pulp-to-tooth area ratio using cone-beam computed tomography: A preliminary analysis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>150-154</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/0975-1475.195118</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Age estimation of living or deceased individuals is an important aspect of forensic sciences. Conventionally, pulp-to-tooth area ratio (PTR) measured from periapical radiographs have been utilized as a nondestructive method of age estimation. Cone-beam computed tomography (CBCT) is a new method to acquire three-dimensional images of the teeth in living individuals.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The present study investigated age estimation based on PTR of the maxillary canines measured in three planes obtained from CBCT image data.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTINGS AND DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sixty subjects aged 20-85 years were included in the study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">For each tooth, mid-sagittal, mid-coronal, and three axial sections-cementoenamel junction (CEJ), one-fourth root level from CEJ, and mid-root-were assessed. PTR was calculated using AutoCAD software after outlining the pulp and tooth.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STATISTICAL ANALYSIS USED" NlmCategory="METHODS">All statistical analyses were performed using an SPSS 17.0 software program.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS AND CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Linear regression analysis showed that only PTR in axial plane at CEJ had significant age correlation (r = 0.32; P &lt; 0.05). This is probably because of clearer demarcation of pulp and tooth outline at this level.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
